  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 501 AM MDT Wed Oct 21 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nToday over Southwestern Montana there remains Southwest flow aloft \nwith weak zonal flow (west-east) over North-central, and Central \nMontana. Due to slightly stronger warm air advection today \ntemperatures will rebound to above seasonal averages. Across North-\ncentral and Central Montana it will be dry with a low chance for \nshowers across Southwestern Montana today. See the Forecast \nConfidence & Scenarios Section for details. On Thursday there \nremains southerly flow aloft and an upper-level trough associated \nwith a Pacific Cold front begins to move over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana by Thursday evening. This will \nallow temperatures to remain above seasonal averages with mostly dry \nweather until Thursday evening. Thursday evening into Friday morning \nprecipitation will gradually spread over North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. \n\nOn Friday the upper-level trough merges with a shortwave trough \nmoving south from Canada. This combined with the cold front that \nmoved through will bring widespread precipitation and cooler \ntemperatures (below seasonal averages) to the area. Due to the \ncold front that moved through Thursday evening into Friday morning\nsnow-levels will lower and the precipitation that falls in \nmountain areas on Friday will transition from rain to snow by \nFriday evening. On Friday there is an increasing pressure gradient\nand strong low-level and mid-level winds. This will bring windy \nconditions to North-central Montana on Friday. On Saturday an \nupper-level ridge begins to build in over North-central, Central, \nand Southwestern Montana. This will allow the area to dry out with\ncool temperatures lingering. \n\nOn Sunday the upper-level ridge remains over North-central, Central, \nand Southwestern Montana. This will keep dry and cool (below \nseasonal averages) weather for the area on Sunday. Monday through \nTuesday an upper-level trough moves over North-central, Central, \nand Southwestern Montana. This will bring cooler temperatures and\nwidespread precipitation to the area on Monday and Tuesday. -IG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nToday across Southwestern Montana along the Idaho/Montana Border \nthere is a 10% chance for a tenth of rain or greater. On Friday \nthere is a 40 - 65% chance for wind gusts of 45 mph or greater \nacross much of North-central Montana. On Friday along the Rocky \nMountain Front and plains west of I-15 there is a 30 - 50% chance \nfor wind gusts of 55 mph or greater. \n\nFor total liquid precipitation Thursday night through Friday night \nthere is greater than an 80% chance for one half inch of liquid \nprecipitation or greater along and south of the Montana Highway \n200/US Highway 87 Corridor between Great Falls and Lewistown. For \ntotal liquid precipitation Thursday night through Friday night there \nis a 30 - 60% chance for one half inch of liquid precipitation \nacross North-central Montana north of the Montana Highway 200/US \nHighway 87 Corridor between Great Falls and Lewistown. For total \nliquid precipitation Thursday night through Friday night there is a \n60 - 90% chance for one inch of liquid precipitation or greater \nacross Central and Southwestern Montana. Across most of the \nmountains of North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana there \nis greater than a 60% chance for 2 inches of snow or greater from \nThursday night through Friday night. Thursday night through \nFriday night there is a 50 - 80% chance for 4 inches of snow or \ngreater across the Madison and Gallatin ranges and the Little \nBelts. Friday afternoon into early Saturday morning there is a \nhigh chance that at least some lower-elevation locations across \nNorth-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana will see a few \nflakes of snow. -IG",
